WEBVTT

00:00:00.114 --> 00:00:04.000
A general assistant needs balanced instruction following, writing, knowledge,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.100
conversational behavior, and safe handling of uncertainty.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.969
A compact model may be sufficient for routine local help,

00:00:09.969 --> 00:00:13.140
while difficult analysis may justify a larger option.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.785
Coding assistants should be tested on the actual languages, repositories, context sizes,

00:00:17.785 --> 00:00:19.590
completion style, debugging, and tool workflow.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:23.805
Benchmark correctness and edit quality rather than relying on generic chat results.

00:00:23.805 --> 00:00:27.620
Writing and editing need tone control, preservation of meaning, revision quality,

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.367
and consistent formatting.

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:33.065
Cybersecurity analysis needs careful evidence handling, domain vocabulary,

00:00:33.065 --> 00:00:37.285
defensive framing, and low tolerance for invented indicators or commands.

00:00:37.285 --> 00:00:40.400
Document review and retrieval need source grounding, citation behavior,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.310
extraction accuracy, and long-context discipline.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.900
Private-assistant use adds requirements for logs, access, local integrations,

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.980
and data retention beyond model quality.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.325
Vision-language workloads need image understanding, visual-token budget, OCR, counting,

00:00:53.325 --> 00:00:56.565
chart or screenshot performance, and additional memory.

00:00:56.565 --> 00:01:00.020
Automation and agents need tool selection, structured outputs, error recovery,

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:02.370
and resistance to compounding mistakes.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:06.610
Select model families by demonstrated task fit, license, runtime support, language needs,

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:07.645
and operational behavior.

00:01:07.645 --> 00:01:11.680
Maintain a fallback when one model is too slow, too weak, or unavailable.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.260
One model need not serve every workload.
